WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.150
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.080
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.870
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.680
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.270
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.230
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.870
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.209 --> 00:00:24.009
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.600
are going to be talking about the

00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:28.469
unexpected return of nuclear power.

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:30.819
It's a story about technology,

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:33.620
energy, politics, and the question

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.810
of how to power the future.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.319
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.879
minute and get right into it.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.000
Politicians don't tend to be particularly

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.510
good at admitting they were wrong.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.579
When your career depends on voters

00:00:51.589 --> 00:00:54.359
believing that you are the best person to

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.890
lead the country, saying that you should

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.440
have done something differently is hard.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:00.939
It's uncomfortable.

00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:04.019
Sometimes, though, it happens.

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:07.599
In January of this year, January of

00:01:07.599 --> 00:01:11.099
2026, the German Chancellor, Friedrich

00:01:11.099 --> 00:01:14.030
Merz, stood in front of the Chamber

00:01:14.209 --> 00:01:17.059
of Industry and Commerce in Dessau and

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:20.309
admitted, in no uncertain terms, that

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.929
his country, Germany, had made a mistake.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.559
To quote him exactly, "It was a

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:30.759
serious strategic mistake to exit

00:01:30.809 --> 00:01:33.379
nuclear energy… we simply don’t have

00:01:33.379 --> 00:01:35.389
enough energy generation capacity."

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:39.969
As you may know, Germany now has

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:42.589
no active nuclear power plants.

00:01:43.089 --> 00:01:46.999
In the 1990s, around 30% of Germany's

00:01:47.029 --> 00:01:49.679
electricity came from nuclear sources.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.940
Today, that number is zero.

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:55.849
And many countries, especially

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:58.089
in Europe, have gone a similar

00:01:58.089 --> 00:02:00.839
route: initial excitement in the

00:02:00.849 --> 00:02:03.309
post-war period about this "new" and

00:02:03.359 --> 00:02:05.369
revolutionary technology, and then a

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:08.389
turn towards protest and opposition.

00:02:09.439 --> 00:02:12.259
In many ways, it was hardly surprising.

00:02:12.718 --> 00:02:14.599
There were question marks over

00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:16.299
what to do with nuclear waste.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:19.289
In 1986, there was Chernobyl.

00:02:19.729 --> 00:02:21.539
And twenty-five years later,

00:02:21.919 --> 00:02:24.479
in 2011, came Fukushima.

00:02:25.489 --> 00:02:27.749
Nuclear power was for many

00:02:27.749 --> 00:02:30.329
people simply too dangerous.

00:02:30.539 --> 00:02:32.749
A risk not worth taking.

00:02:33.749 --> 00:02:36.039
But then, in the past couple of

00:02:36.039 --> 00:02:38.569
years, something has changed.

00:02:39.019 --> 00:02:41.019
People have started talking about a

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:44.619
new nuclear age, a nuclear renaissance,

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:46.899
where nuclear energy is treated not

00:02:46.949 --> 00:02:49.319
as "the answer" to how we power the

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:52.126
planet, but as "part of the answer".

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:56.669
And in some cases, this has resulted

00:02:56.679 --> 00:02:59.469
in some spectacular U-turns.

00:03:00.389 --> 00:03:02.649
Belgium is one example.

00:03:03.109 --> 00:03:06.779
In 2003, Belgium passed a law

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:08.789
committing itself to shutting down

00:03:09.239 --> 00:03:11.809
every nuclear reactor in the country.

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:15.799
It was broadly the same story as Germany:

00:03:16.189 --> 00:03:19.089
public fear after Chernobyl, a strong

00:03:19.169 --> 00:03:22.479
Green movement, and a political consensus

00:03:22.699 --> 00:03:25.789
that nuclear power belonged to the past.

00:03:26.709 --> 00:03:28.969
By last year, Belgium was

00:03:28.969 --> 00:03:29.929
most of the way there.

00:03:30.679 --> 00:03:32.949
Plants had been scheduled for closure.

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:36.229
Engineers had already begun the paperwork

00:03:36.229 --> 00:03:38.719
for decommissioning, which is the long,

00:03:38.919 --> 00:03:41.179
careful process of shutting a reactor

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:43.809
down for good and making it safe.

00:03:44.889 --> 00:03:48.419
And then, in May 2025, the

00:03:48.419 --> 00:03:50.239
Belgian parliament did something

00:03:50.239 --> 00:03:51.499
people weren't expecting.

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:54.599
It repealed the law completely,

00:03:55.059 --> 00:03:57.859
cancelling it as if it had never existed.

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:01.499
And the government didn't just decide

00:04:01.509 --> 00:04:03.489
to keep the remaining reactors running.

00:04:03.999 --> 00:04:06.829
It announced it wanted to extend the

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:09.129
lifespans of the ones that had already

00:04:09.129 --> 00:04:11.399
been prepared for shutdown, and it

00:04:11.399 --> 00:04:14.109
started looking at building new ones.

00:04:14.649 --> 00:04:17.289
In other words, a full reversal: from

00:04:17.659 --> 00:04:20.129
"we're shutting it all down" to "we're not

00:04:20.129 --> 00:04:23.119
doing that AND we're building new ones".

00:04:24.119 --> 00:04:25.759
Belgium wasn't even the

00:04:25.759 --> 00:04:27.339
most extreme example.

00:04:27.989 --> 00:04:30.639
Italy banned nuclear power after

00:04:30.639 --> 00:04:33.239
Chernobyl, and confirmed that ban

00:04:33.449 --> 00:04:36.789
again after Fukushima, when 94% of

00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:39.639
voters rejected building new reactors.

00:04:40.279 --> 00:04:42.489
Its last two reactors had

00:04:42.489 --> 00:04:44.689
already closed, back in 1990.

00:04:45.471 --> 00:04:49.013
But in October of last year, October

00:04:49.013 --> 00:04:52.509
2025, Italy's government approved

00:04:52.529 --> 00:04:55.249
a new bill to bring nuclear power

00:04:55.259 --> 00:04:57.779
back: small modular reactors,

00:04:57.999 --> 00:05:01.059
new-generation designs, and even fusion.

00:05:01.219 --> 00:05:02.466
The whole shebang.

00:05:03.629 --> 00:05:06.269
And it's a similar change of mood

00:05:06.299 --> 00:05:09.549
in the US, in Japan, the world over.

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:13.289
Even in countries that haven't reversed

00:05:13.319 --> 00:05:16.799
course entirely, the tone has changed.

00:05:17.489 --> 00:05:19.419
In many countries, nuclear power

00:05:19.609 --> 00:05:22.029
has become a non-partisan issue:

00:05:22.339 --> 00:05:24.779
something those on the right and the

00:05:24.779 --> 00:05:27.499
left agree on, and even something

00:05:27.499 --> 00:05:29.929
that some Green parties have started

00:05:29.929 --> 00:05:32.665
to come round to as an important tool

00:05:33.012 --> 00:05:34.978
in the fight against climate change.

00:05:35.798 --> 00:05:38.097
So what changed?

00:05:38.098 --> 00:05:40.269
Well, first, there's the

00:05:40.289 --> 00:05:42.509
question of energy security.

00:05:43.189 --> 00:05:45.719
After the Russian invasion of Ukraine,

00:05:45.949 --> 00:05:48.309
it became clear that relying on cheap

00:05:48.529 --> 00:05:51.469
Russian gas hadn't been the smartest move.

00:05:51.869 --> 00:05:53.269
Or in fact relying on

00:05:53.439 --> 00:05:54.659
anyone else for that matter.

00:05:55.429 --> 00:05:58.169
To maintain acceptable prices and

00:05:58.169 --> 00:06:00.789
reliable supply, countries started to

00:06:00.789 --> 00:06:03.169
admit that being able to produce your

00:06:03.259 --> 00:06:05.899
own energy was extremely important.

00:06:07.279 --> 00:06:09.479
Second, there's the climate question.

00:06:10.249 --> 00:06:12.759
For a long time, the assumption

00:06:12.759 --> 00:06:14.889
was that renewables like wind

00:06:15.128 --> 00:06:16.818
and solar could be enough.

00:06:16.999 --> 00:06:18.459
Enough wind turbines and

00:06:18.459 --> 00:06:20.729
solar panels could deliver all

00:06:20.729 --> 00:06:22.169
the energy a country needed.

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:26.059
It's a nice idea, but for most

00:06:26.089 --> 00:06:28.159
countries, it doesn't quite work.

00:06:28.579 --> 00:06:30.759
Wind and solar are cheap, and

00:06:30.769 --> 00:06:32.269
they are quick and easy to build,

00:06:32.439 --> 00:06:34.299
relative to nuclear at least.

00:06:34.859 --> 00:06:37.099
But the sun doesn't always shine,

00:06:37.429 --> 00:06:39.299
and the wind doesn't always blow.

00:06:40.009 --> 00:06:42.929
Solar might be able to power the entire

00:06:42.929 --> 00:06:45.459
grid in the middle of the day, but not

00:06:45.519 --> 00:06:48.079
at night or on a dark winter's day.

00:06:48.789 --> 00:06:51.389
And while batteries are getting cheaper

00:06:51.499 --> 00:06:54.709
every year, storing enough electricity

00:06:54.869 --> 00:06:56.449
to keep a country running for days

00:06:56.449 --> 00:06:59.359
at a time is still a long way off.

00:07:00.729 --> 00:07:02.749
Nuclear power, on the other hand,

00:07:03.009 --> 00:07:04.969
doesn't care whether the sun is

00:07:04.969 --> 00:07:07.159
shining or the wind is blowing.

00:07:07.939 --> 00:07:10.819
Grid engineers call this baseload

00:07:10.849 --> 00:07:14.259
power: a steady, reliable supply that

00:07:14.259 --> 00:07:16.519
is always there, whatever the weather.

00:07:17.289 --> 00:07:20.019
And increasingly, governments have

00:07:20.019 --> 00:07:23.559
concluded that nuclear and renewables need

00:07:23.609 --> 00:07:26.309
to work together, with nuclear providing

00:07:26.309 --> 00:07:28.899
the steady backbone that a renewable-heavy

00:07:28.899 --> 00:07:30.959
grid needs to actually function.

00:07:32.149 --> 00:07:35.289
And after it's up and running, nuclear

00:07:35.289 --> 00:07:38.469
power produces almost no carbon emissions.

00:07:39.189 --> 00:07:42.469
Now, not all Green parties agree with

00:07:42.489 --> 00:07:45.469
nuclear yet, but it's starting to change.

00:07:46.099 --> 00:07:49.379
In Finland, the Green party voted

00:07:49.379 --> 00:07:52.739
back in 2022 to formally support

00:07:52.759 --> 00:07:55.019
nuclear power as part of its climate

00:07:55.019 --> 00:07:57.769
strategy, and the tone has started to

00:07:57.769 --> 00:07:59.839
change in several other countries too.

00:08:00.909 --> 00:08:03.769
So you have the energy security question

00:08:03.969 --> 00:08:06.429
and the environmental one, but there's

00:08:06.429 --> 00:08:09.729
another factor: demand for electricity is

00:08:09.809 --> 00:08:13.229
booming, and this is largely coming from

00:08:13.229 --> 00:08:15.409
the technology sitting in your pocket.

00:08:16.049 --> 00:08:18.149
Data centres in the United States

00:08:18.339 --> 00:08:20.589
now use more than 4% of the

00:08:20.589 --> 00:08:23.049
country's entire electricity supply.

00:08:23.689 --> 00:08:26.899
A data centre is a huge building full of

00:08:26.899 --> 00:08:29.659
computer servers, the machines that store

00:08:29.829 --> 00:08:32.619
and process information for the internet.

00:08:33.239 --> 00:08:34.769
They've been around for decades,

00:08:35.099 --> 00:08:37.549
and despite what the fear mongering

00:08:37.549 --> 00:08:39.639
might have you believe, most of

00:08:39.639 --> 00:08:41.459
what they do has nothing to do

00:08:41.459 --> 00:08:42.989
with artificial intelligence.

00:08:43.379 --> 00:08:45.299
They're where your Netflix movies are

00:08:45.299 --> 00:08:47.879
stored, your Spotify playlists are

00:08:47.889 --> 00:08:50.649
saved, and where the tens of billions

00:08:50.649 --> 00:08:53.139
of hours of YouTube videos are held.

00:08:53.609 --> 00:08:56.189
And yes, this episode and my

00:08:56.189 --> 00:08:58.059
voice today are brought to you

00:08:58.309 --> 00:09:00.319
courtesy of your local data centre.

00:09:01.409 --> 00:09:02.539
They are what companies talk

00:09:02.549 --> 00:09:04.939
about when they say "the cloud".

00:09:05.209 --> 00:09:07.579
It's not a cloud, it's a huge warehouse

00:09:07.579 --> 00:09:10.009
in Arizona or somewhere like that.

00:09:11.199 --> 00:09:13.839
And for most of the last twenty

00:09:13.839 --> 00:09:15.949
years, these buildings have been

00:09:15.949 --> 00:09:18.389
growing steadily but predictably.

00:09:19.079 --> 00:09:22.559
Then came generative AI which, as

00:09:22.559 --> 00:09:24.769
I'm sure you know, or might remember

00:09:24.819 --> 00:09:28.269
from episode 585, has much more

00:09:28.289 --> 00:09:30.359
intensive energy requirements.

00:09:30.979 --> 00:09:33.239
And with billions of people using

00:09:33.259 --> 00:09:35.719
AI, well, you get a level of

00:09:35.719 --> 00:09:37.489
electricity demand that didn't

00:09:37.499 --> 00:09:39.319
really exist five years ago.

00:09:40.109 --> 00:09:42.799
To put it in real terms, Goldman

00:09:42.799 --> 00:09:45.239
Sachs now projects that electricity

00:09:45.239 --> 00:09:47.739
demand from data centres will grow by

00:09:47.739 --> 00:09:53.029
160% by 2030, driven largely by AI.

00:09:54.089 --> 00:09:56.669
It means that within a few years,

00:09:56.999 --> 00:09:59.109
the amount of power these buildings

00:09:59.109 --> 00:10:01.619
need could be more than double what

00:10:01.619 --> 00:10:03.879
it is today, on top of everything

00:10:03.879 --> 00:10:05.708
else the grid has to supply.

00:10:06.759 --> 00:10:08.629
These data centres need

00:10:08.679 --> 00:10:11.129
reliable, 24/7 energy.

00:10:11.906 --> 00:10:13.576
Technology companies have actually

00:10:13.576 --> 00:10:15.876
been some of the world's biggest buyers

00:10:15.876 --> 00:10:18.186
of wind and solar power for years,

00:10:18.516 --> 00:10:21.336
but renewables alone cannot promise

00:10:21.356 --> 00:10:23.396
power at every hour of every day.

00:10:24.996 --> 00:10:26.286
And, one more thing.

00:10:26.876 --> 00:10:29.136
Even once a data centre is built

00:10:29.456 --> 00:10:31.466
and ready to be turned on, you

00:10:31.466 --> 00:10:33.476
don't just plug it into the grid.

00:10:33.986 --> 00:10:36.426
There are long waiting lists to ensure

00:10:36.486 --> 00:10:38.566
that a new customer won't overload the

00:10:38.566 --> 00:10:41.456
local network, and in some parts of the

00:10:41.456 --> 00:10:44.606
United States, these waiting lists stretch

00:10:44.606 --> 00:10:47.186
to eight years, which is eight years

00:10:47.186 --> 00:10:49.756
too long if you're a technology company

00:10:49.776 --> 00:10:52.451
racing to build the next generation of AI.

00:10:53.766 --> 00:10:56.226
So the world's biggest technology

00:10:56.226 --> 00:10:58.946
companies did something unexpected,

00:10:59.226 --> 00:11:01.456
or I guess rather obvious when

00:11:01.456 --> 00:11:02.946
you understand that electricity

00:11:02.966 --> 00:11:04.283
is their biggest constraint.

00:11:04.956 --> 00:11:06.526
They decided to become

00:11:06.626 --> 00:11:08.546
energy companies themselves.

00:11:09.346 --> 00:11:11.076
And here, enter stage

00:11:11.076 --> 00:11:12.926
right, nuclear power.

00:11:13.866 --> 00:11:16.186
Microsoft, for example, signed a

00:11:16.216 --> 00:11:19.356
deal back in 2024 to bring one of the

00:11:19.356 --> 00:11:22.026
two reactors at Three Mile Island,

00:11:22.046 --> 00:11:24.196
in Pennsylvania, back to life.

00:11:24.976 --> 00:11:27.056
As you may remember, Three Mile

00:11:27.096 --> 00:11:29.256
Island was the site of a famous

00:11:29.356 --> 00:11:33.026
partial meltdown back in 1979, but

00:11:33.296 --> 00:11:35.256
that happened in the other reactor.

00:11:36.036 --> 00:11:38.826
This one ran safely for decades,

00:11:38.916 --> 00:11:41.616
and only closed in 2019, for

00:11:41.776 --> 00:11:43.386
ordinary financial reasons.

00:11:44.246 --> 00:11:46.996
The plant's owner, an energy company

00:11:47.046 --> 00:11:49.257
called Constellation, is spending

00:11:49.267 --> 00:11:52.837
$1.6 billion to refurbish it,

00:11:53.127 --> 00:11:55.858
and Microsoft has agreed to buy

00:11:56.164 --> 00:11:58.908
100% of the electricity the plant

00:11:58.908 --> 00:12:01.548
produces for the next twenty years.

00:12:02.278 --> 00:12:04.918
In other words, it effectively gets its

00:12:04.918 --> 00:12:07.968
own nuclear reactor, and all of the energy

00:12:08.108 --> 00:12:11.098
it produces is earmarked for data centres.

00:12:11.758 --> 00:12:13.068
And it's a similar thing

00:12:13.068 --> 00:12:15.538
with Meta, Amazon, Google,

00:12:15.878 --> 00:12:17.348
all the big tech companies.

00:12:17.778 --> 00:12:20.688
Some are restarting old plants, others

00:12:20.688 --> 00:12:22.918
are buying capacity from existing plants,

00:12:23.258 --> 00:12:25.818
others are investing in companies building

00:12:25.828 --> 00:12:28.538
small modular reactors, which are, as

00:12:28.538 --> 00:12:31.268
the name suggests, smaller reactors that

00:12:31.268 --> 00:12:33.878
are built off-site and then assembled,

00:12:34.118 --> 00:12:36.778
a bit like IKEA furniture, but with, I

00:12:36.808 --> 00:12:39.208
trust, slightly more helpful instructions.

00:12:39.828 --> 00:12:42.328
Add it all together, and the nuclear

00:12:42.328 --> 00:12:44.978
capacity now committed to data centres

00:12:45.168 --> 00:12:48.294
by these companies is up to 13 gigawatts,

00:12:48.388 --> 00:12:50.878
and that is just in the United States.

00:12:51.608 --> 00:12:53.748
To put it in context, this is

00:12:53.758 --> 00:12:56.078
more electricity than the entire

00:12:56.088 --> 00:12:57.888
Netherlands uses in a year.

00:12:58.758 --> 00:12:59.548
I'm not sure if that

00:12:59.548 --> 00:13:01.768
sounds like a lot or not.

00:13:02.138 --> 00:13:03.838
It's a lot when you think that it's

00:13:03.838 --> 00:13:06.408
like the electricity used by 18 million

00:13:06.408 --> 00:13:08.548
people, but if you think that this

00:13:08.568 --> 00:13:11.068
is powering products used by a large

00:13:11.068 --> 00:13:12.608
proportion of the world's population,

00:13:13.288 --> 00:13:14.898
perhaps it seems more reasonable.

00:13:15.878 --> 00:13:18.828
In any case, never before has so

00:13:18.828 --> 00:13:21.068
much private money been committed

00:13:21.138 --> 00:13:23.198
to nuclear energy, and we are

00:13:23.198 --> 00:13:25.638
only six years into the decade.

00:13:26.428 --> 00:13:29.708
For decades, nuclear power depended

00:13:29.798 --> 00:13:32.518
almost entirely on governments: government

00:13:32.518 --> 00:13:35.048
funding, government regulation, government

00:13:35.058 --> 00:13:37.768
risk-taking, because private investors

00:13:37.988 --> 00:13:40.448
found it too expensive and too slow.

00:13:41.108 --> 00:13:44.138
Now it's Microsoft, Meta, Amazon,

00:13:44.237 --> 00:13:45.677
and Google that are bankrolling

00:13:45.708 --> 00:13:47.388
reactors, providing the money

00:13:47.648 --> 00:13:48.948
that governments once did.

00:13:49.508 --> 00:13:51.788
These are companies whose main business

00:13:52.018 --> 00:13:53.898
has nothing to do with electricity.

00:13:54.359 --> 00:13:56.918
But the alternative is running out

00:13:56.918 --> 00:13:59.058
of power to sell their own products.

00:14:00.058 --> 00:14:02.208
Of course, not everyone is

00:14:02.208 --> 00:14:03.758
comfortable with that shift.

00:14:04.138 --> 00:14:06.357
Critics point out that decisions made

00:14:06.377 --> 00:14:08.278
about something as safety-critical

00:14:08.488 --> 00:14:10.958
as nuclear power are increasingly

00:14:11.258 --> 00:14:13.268
being made by companies whose main

00:14:13.278 --> 00:14:15.268
interest is keeping their data centres

00:14:15.268 --> 00:14:17.498
running, not public accountability.

00:14:18.518 --> 00:14:20.278
Now, there are a few more

00:14:20.308 --> 00:14:21.628
things to talk about.

00:14:22.148 --> 00:14:25.398
Firstly, the financing of nuclear plants.

00:14:26.038 --> 00:14:28.088
One of the big criticisms of

00:14:28.088 --> 00:14:29.858
nuclear power, historically at

00:14:29.858 --> 00:14:31.948
least, is that it is expensive.

00:14:32.518 --> 00:14:34.708
Sure, once you get a plant up and

00:14:34.708 --> 00:14:37.738
running, it's cheap to run, but the

00:14:37.748 --> 00:14:40.198
build of it is incredibly expensive,

00:14:40.478 --> 00:14:43.158
and costs almost always overrun,

00:14:43.568 --> 00:14:45.498
or perhaps spiral out of control

00:14:45.658 --> 00:14:47.028
would be a better way of putting it.

00:14:47.967 --> 00:14:50.488
That is still true, but there are

00:14:50.488 --> 00:14:52.468
a few things worth mentioning.

00:14:53.188 --> 00:14:56.628
A large cost of building a full-scale

00:14:56.628 --> 00:14:58.778
nuclear plant is the cost of

00:14:59.098 --> 00:15:01.538
financing it, the cost of capital.

00:15:02.478 --> 00:15:05.338
If banks and investors provide you

00:15:05.338 --> 00:15:09.538
with €1 billion in financing at a 10%

00:15:09.568 --> 00:15:12.368
interest rate, let's say, that's €100

00:15:12.418 --> 00:15:15.748
million in interest payments every year.

00:15:16.388 --> 00:15:18.018
Let's say, for the sake of example,

00:15:18.318 --> 00:15:20.568
a nuclear power plant takes 10 years

00:15:20.568 --> 00:15:24.108
to build, you will pay €1 billion in

00:15:24.178 --> 00:15:26.958
interest payments on a €1 billion loan.

00:15:28.188 --> 00:15:30.148
And the numbers are often

00:15:30.198 --> 00:15:31.848
much worse than that.

00:15:32.488 --> 00:15:36.858
In 2016, the UK approved a nuclear

00:15:36.858 --> 00:15:39.048
power station called Hinkley Point

00:15:39.058 --> 00:15:41.198
C, in the south-west of England.

00:15:41.788 --> 00:15:43.728
Now, plans had actually started

00:15:43.728 --> 00:15:45.458
for this plant as early as

00:15:45.458 --> 00:15:48.288
1981, but that's another issue.

00:15:49.188 --> 00:15:51.658
Anyway, its initial projected

00:15:51.658 --> 00:15:55.188
cost was £18 billion, and it was

00:15:55.218 --> 00:15:59.008
expected to come online in 2025.

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:03.248
As of today, mid-2026, its

00:16:03.268 --> 00:16:07.408
projected cost is £48 billion in

00:16:07.608 --> 00:16:10.308
inflation-adjusted terms, and it's not

00:16:10.348 --> 00:16:13.968
scheduled to come online before 2030.

00:16:14.918 --> 00:16:16.378
There are a few things

00:16:16.378 --> 00:16:17.308
to talk about here then.

00:16:17.738 --> 00:16:19.908
The first is that big technology

00:16:19.908 --> 00:16:22.288
companies are still investing in nuclear

00:16:22.308 --> 00:16:25.438
energy despite these frequent cost

00:16:25.438 --> 00:16:27.798
overruns, which gives you some idea of

00:16:27.798 --> 00:16:29.748
just how desperate they are for power.

00:16:30.728 --> 00:16:33.068
And the second is that governments are

00:16:33.068 --> 00:16:36.408
now stepping in to help the financing of

00:16:36.448 --> 00:16:38.918
new nuclear power stations, guaranteeing

00:16:38.918 --> 00:16:41.738
the price for a set period, or offering

00:16:41.768 --> 00:16:43.808
a more manageable interest rate.

00:16:44.978 --> 00:16:47.788
And small modular reactors, which

00:16:47.958 --> 00:16:50.058
I mentioned a few minutes ago, are

00:16:50.118 --> 00:16:52.288
another way to fight the issues that

00:16:52.288 --> 00:16:54.168
have plagued the construction of

00:16:54.168 --> 00:16:56.068
these large nuclear power plants.

00:16:56.847 --> 00:16:59.108
The fact that they are smaller means

00:16:59.108 --> 00:17:01.488
they are cheaper to build, faster to put

00:17:01.488 --> 00:17:04.338
up, and easier to place close to where

00:17:04.338 --> 00:17:06.038
the electricity is actually needed,

00:17:06.218 --> 00:17:07.898
like right next to a data centre.

00:17:08.668 --> 00:17:10.508
At least, that's the idea.

00:17:11.128 --> 00:17:14.148
This is still new technology, largely a

00:17:14.198 --> 00:17:17.008
technology of blueprints and prototypes

00:17:17.228 --> 00:17:19.198
rather than working power stations.

00:17:19.838 --> 00:17:23.827
But 2026 has been the year SMRs,

00:17:23.947 --> 00:17:25.509
the small modular reactors,

00:17:26.136 --> 00:17:27.636
they started to look real.

00:17:28.608 --> 00:17:31.068
There's an SMR in China which is

00:17:31.078 --> 00:17:33.188
scheduled to start commercial operation

00:17:33.478 --> 00:17:35.608
in the next 12 months, and others

00:17:35.828 --> 00:17:38.248
being built in Canada and America.

00:17:39.527 --> 00:17:40.968
Still, 'have heard this

00:17:41.008 --> 00:17:42.118
kind of story before.

00:17:42.668 --> 00:17:44.968
Nuclear power has a long history

00:17:44.968 --> 00:17:47.078
of projects that were promised on

00:17:47.078 --> 00:17:49.178
time and on budget, and then arrived

00:17:49.208 --> 00:17:51.398
years late and billions over.

00:17:52.128 --> 00:17:54.758
Sceptics point out that SMRs are,

00:17:54.758 --> 00:17:57.488
so far, mostly a story told in press

00:17:57.488 --> 00:17:59.168
releases and government funding

00:17:59.168 --> 00:18:01.348
announcements, not in electricity

00:18:01.831 --> 00:18:03.318
actually flowing onto the grid.

00:18:04.458 --> 00:18:06.418
And there's also the question

00:18:06.668 --> 00:18:08.318
that has haunted nuclear power

00:18:08.518 --> 00:18:11.488
for seventy years: nuclear waste.

00:18:12.598 --> 00:18:15.128
Anyone who grew up watching The Simpsons

00:18:15.508 --> 00:18:17.968
probably remembers Homer walking into

00:18:17.968 --> 00:18:20.628
his job at a nuclear power plant, and

00:18:20.798 --> 00:18:23.188
there being big drums of fluorescent

00:18:23.198 --> 00:18:26.358
green slime that gives fish three eyes.

00:18:27.168 --> 00:18:29.438
Of course, spent nuclear fuel

00:18:29.598 --> 00:18:31.708
is dangerous, and it remains

00:18:31.708 --> 00:18:34.098
radioactive for thousands of years.

00:18:34.548 --> 00:18:37.678
But there has been significant investment

00:18:37.918 --> 00:18:40.678
and progress in solving this issue.

00:18:41.468 --> 00:18:43.968
Recently, Finland has shown

00:18:44.018 --> 00:18:46.208
that the problem can be solved.

00:18:46.838 --> 00:18:49.548
It is in the final stages of opening a

00:18:49.548 --> 00:18:52.178
nuclear storage site deep underground

00:18:52.488 --> 00:18:54.138
that will be able to safely store

00:18:54.148 --> 00:18:57.428
nuclear waste for 100,000 years.

00:18:58.788 --> 00:19:01.068
And there is also the promise, or at

00:19:01.068 --> 00:19:04.008
least the allure, of nuclear fusion,

00:19:04.248 --> 00:19:06.168
the other kind of nuclear power, the

00:19:06.168 --> 00:19:08.598
one that works by fusing atoms together

00:19:08.918 --> 00:19:10.598
rather than splitting them apart.

00:19:11.818 --> 00:19:14.618
Fusion has been "twenty years away"

00:19:14.778 --> 00:19:17.968
for about seventy years, and 2026

00:19:18.108 --> 00:19:20.368
hasn't quite broken that pattern.

00:19:20.848 --> 00:19:23.618
But something has changed here too.

00:19:24.308 --> 00:19:26.498
More than fourteen billion dollars

00:19:26.698 --> 00:19:28.878
in investment has gone into fusion

00:19:28.878 --> 00:19:31.418
companies, and almost a third of

00:19:31.418 --> 00:19:34.228
that arrived in the past year alone.

00:19:34.888 --> 00:19:37.248
Microsoft has signed a deal to

00:19:37.248 --> 00:19:39.218
buy fusion electricity from a

00:19:39.218 --> 00:19:41.858
company called Helion by 2028.

00:19:42.318 --> 00:19:44.718
And regulators in the US have

00:19:44.718 --> 00:19:47.098
started treating fusion as a properly

00:19:47.118 --> 00:19:49.308
separate category from traditional

00:19:49.328 --> 00:19:51.818
nuclear power, with lighter rules,

00:19:51.908 --> 00:19:54.028
because a fusion reactor can't melt

00:19:54.038 --> 00:19:56.258
down the way a fission reactor can.

00:19:57.708 --> 00:20:00.548
If nuclear fusion can work, and

00:20:00.728 --> 00:20:03.208
clearly there is a lot of smart money

00:20:03.298 --> 00:20:05.656
betting that it can, the economics of

00:20:05.666 --> 00:20:08.106
nuclear power change significantly.

00:20:09.076 --> 00:20:11.756
In a traditional nuclear power station,

00:20:12.016 --> 00:20:15.096
the fuel keeps producing heat even after

00:20:15.096 --> 00:20:17.226
the reactor has been switched off, and

00:20:17.336 --> 00:20:20.076
it has to be kept cool at all times.

00:20:20.936 --> 00:20:23.216
At Fukushima, the reactors had

00:20:23.236 --> 00:20:25.546
already shut themselves down, but the

00:20:25.586 --> 00:20:28.266
cooling failed, and the fuel melted.

00:20:28.726 --> 00:20:31.436
This is why fission plants need so

00:20:31.456 --> 00:20:34.136
many layers of safety systems, and

00:20:34.356 --> 00:20:36.686
those layers are a big part of what

00:20:36.686 --> 00:20:38.356
makes them so expensive to build.

00:20:39.156 --> 00:20:41.486
A fusion reaction has the

00:20:41.546 --> 00:20:43.676
opposite problem: it is incredibly

00:20:43.676 --> 00:20:45.196
difficult to keep going.

00:20:45.526 --> 00:20:47.666
It only happens under extreme

00:20:47.666 --> 00:20:50.646
conditions, and there is only ever a

00:20:50.776 --> 00:20:53.076
tiny amount of fuel in the machine.

00:20:53.866 --> 00:20:56.576
If anything goes wrong, the conditions

00:20:56.756 --> 00:20:59.376
collapse and the reaction simply stops.

00:20:59.496 --> 00:21:01.276
There is nothing that can melt down.

00:21:02.446 --> 00:21:04.756
That's a large part of why people are

00:21:04.766 --> 00:21:08.076
so excited about fusion: in theory,

00:21:08.406 --> 00:21:10.326
it offers something close to nuclear

00:21:10.326 --> 00:21:13.916
power's upside, of abundant, low-carbon

00:21:13.926 --> 00:21:16.656
electricity, without its biggest danger.

00:21:17.596 --> 00:21:20.546
So, to wrap things up, even a couple

00:21:20.546 --> 00:21:23.286
of years ago, nuclear power was a story

00:21:23.326 --> 00:21:25.836
about a fight between the past and

00:21:25.836 --> 00:21:28.186
the future: old reactors closing in

00:21:28.186 --> 00:21:30.546
Europe while new ones opened in Asia.

00:21:31.156 --> 00:21:33.876
Today, it's a story about the environment

00:21:34.216 --> 00:21:36.646
and about energy sovereignty, not just

00:21:36.656 --> 00:21:39.066
for governments but for big tech too.

00:21:39.766 --> 00:21:43.306
The promise is alluring: cheap, clean,

00:21:43.376 --> 00:21:45.316
abundant electricity for everyone.

00:21:45.866 --> 00:21:47.446
Whether that can really be

00:21:47.446 --> 00:21:49.326
delivered, well, that is the

00:21:49.356 --> 00:21:50.866
trillion-dollar question.

00:21:52.336 --> 00:21:54.756
OK, then, that is it for today's episode

00:21:54.946 --> 00:21:57.726
on the unexpected return of nuclear power.

00:21:58.046 --> 00:21:59.336
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:21:59.576 --> 00:22:01.386
and that you've learnt something new.

00:22:01.806 --> 00:22:03.646
As always, I would love to know

00:22:03.646 --> 00:22:05.116
what you thought of this episode.

00:22:05.336 --> 00:22:06.886
Let me know in the comments below,

00:22:07.056 --> 00:22:08.446
if you're listening to this somewhere

00:22:08.446 --> 00:22:10.296
where you can comment, and for the

00:22:10.296 --> 00:22:12.086
members among you, you can head right

00:22:12.106 --> 00:22:14.086
into our community forum, which is at

00:22:14.086 --> 00:22:16.886
community.leonardoenglish.com and get

00:22:16.886 --> 00:22:19.046
chatting away to other curious minds.

00:22:20.318 --> 00:22:22.138
You've been listening to English Learning

00:22:22.138 --> 00:22:24.868
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:22:25.168 --> 00:22:28.158
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:22:28.198 --> 00:22:30.638
I'll catch you in the next episode.

